# Search before you implement

If the work is complicated — a bug with an unclear cause, an unfamiliar API, a version-sensitive integration, auth, payments, maps, Mongoose, or anything you would have to invent — **search first. Do not write code from memory.**

## Do this first

0. Use Context7 when you need library/API documentation, setup, or configuration (resolve-library-id → query-docs) without being asked.
1. Search current official docs (and GitHub issues / changelogs when the docs are thin).
2. Prefer vendor docs over blogs: Next.js, NextAuth, Mongoose, Stripe, MultiSafepay, Google Maps, next-intl, shadcn, Tailwind, Zod.
3. Use project skills (`.cursor/skills/`) and MCP docs tools when they exist.
4. Confirm the API, env vars, and **version this repo actually uses**.
5. Then implement. Follow the documented pattern — do not improvise a parallel one.

## Counts as complicated (search)

- Auth, sessions, cookies, CSRF
- Stripe PaymentIntents, webhooks, MultiSafepay
- Maps, Places, Distance Matrix, bounds
- Mongoose 9 connection pooling on serverless
- next-intl routing, RTL (`ar`)
- shadcn / Tailwind v4
- Errors you cannot explain from the local code in one pass

## Skip the search

- Rename, copy, wire an existing local pattern, or a change you can see fully in this repo.

## After the search

- If docs disagree with this repo's stack, **this repo wins** (Next.js App Router, Mongoose, NextAuth v4, Zod 4). Adapt the documented approach; do not switch libraries.
